      "id": 1,
      "text": "Greenville was established on former Chickasaw Nation lands that were later opened to non-Native settlement after Oklahoma statehood in 1907.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/greenville_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Greenville is an old settlement that appeared on a 1911 Rand McNally map of Love County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/greenville_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The early economy of Greenville centered on livestock and crops like cotton, grain, and hay grown in the region's rich soils.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/greenville_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Works Progress Administration constructed the Greenville School from native stone between 1937 and 1938 to provide local employment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/greenville_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Greenville remains a rural community where agriculture, including pecans and livestock, continues to be a key economic driver.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/greenville_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Greenville is located in northeastern Love County, situated between the cities of Marietta and Ardmore.",
